Short answer
- Use optimistic UI on the client (show the message immediately with a temporary clientId and "pending" status).
- Persist the message asynchronously on the server and send an acknowledgement event (messageSaved or messageError) back to the client with the final server id and timestamp.
- Use retries for transient DB/network errors, and a message queue or batch writes to reduce write latency and DB load.
- Update the UI when the ack arrives (swap temp id → server id, mark as saved or failed) so the interface stays smooth and responsive.

Why this works
- Optimistic UI gives instant feedback to users while the server does the real work in the background.
- Acknowledgements let you keep client and DB state consistent (and provide a way to retry or surface errors).
- Asynchronous persistence + batching/queueing avoids blocking the Socket.io event loop and reduces DB spikes.

Server-side example (Socket.io + Mongoose + async error handling)
- This shows: optimistic workflow, async save with try/catch, ack back to client. (Adjust models/paths to your project.)

```javascript
// socketHandlers.js
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const Message = require('./models/Message'); // Mongoose model
const retry = require('async-retry');       // optional retry helper

async function saveWithRetry(messageData) {
  return retry(async () => {
    const msg = new Message(messageData);
    return await msg.save();
  }, {
    retries: 3,
    factor: 2,
    minTimeout: 1000,
    onRetry: (err, attempt) => {
      console.warn(`save attempt ${attempt} failed:`, err.message);
    },
  });
}

module.exports = function setupSocket(io) {
  io.on('connection', (socket) => {
    socket.on('sendMessage', async (payload) => {
      // Payload from client should include a clientMessageId (temp id) and content
      // { clientMessageId, roomId, text, senderId }
      const { clientMessageId, roomId, text, senderId } = payload;

      // Optionally broadcast a "messagePending" so other clients see it as pending
      socket.to(roomId).emit('messagePending', {
        clientMessageId,
        roomId,
        text,
        senderId,
        status: 'pending',
        createdAt: Date.now()
      });

      // Save asynchronously (do not block other handlers)
      try {
        const saved = await saveWithRetry({
          text,
          senderId: mongoose.Types.ObjectId(senderId),
          roomId,
          timestamp: new Date(),
        });

        // Emit ack to the originating client so it can replace the temp id + mark "saved"
        socket.emit('messageSaved', {
          clientMessageId,
          serverMessageId: saved._id.toString(),
          timestamp: saved.timestamp || saved.createdAt || Date.now(),
          status: 'saved',
        });

        // Broadcast the finalized message to room members (so others update from pending → saved)
        io.to(roomId).emit('chatMessage', {
          messageId: saved._id.toString(),
          text: saved.text,
          senderId: saved.senderId,
          timestamp: saved.timestamp || saved.createdAt,
        });
      } catch (err) {
        console.error('Failed to persist message:', err);
        // Tell client the save failed so it can show retry/failure UI
        socket.emit('messageError', {
          clientMessageId,
          error: err.message || 'Failed to save message',
        });
        // Optionally notify room that this message failed (if you broadcasted pending earlier)
        io.to(roomId).emit('messageFailed', { clientMessageId });
      }
    });
  });
};
```

Client-side pattern (optimistic UI + ack handling)
- Show the message immediately with a clientMessageId, then update when server ack arrives.

